{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789832184,"nonce":"0Y9iQvbxTBbldY4KW9yPYJtLznc=","title":"method.bind(x) on an instance method calls it on the wrong receiver; a bound Void function returns a missing value","message":"`tryOverflow.bind(a)` inside an instance method built a lambda whose body was `FunctionCall(Variable(tryOverflow), [a])`: no receiver, so the lambda's first env slot (the capture of `a`) was read as `this` and the argument zero-filled -- `use(a)` bound this way received 0, and TestInt64's `exc(tryOverflow.bind(a))` saw no exception. A bound `Void` function's lambda ended with `return \u003cvoid call\u003e`, which Cranelift refused (\"Return value IrId(N) not found\") and LLVM turned into `ret i8 0` in a void function.\n\nFix: a bare instance method of the enclosing class binds as `MethodCall(this, method, args)` so `this` is captured like any other call; a Void function's body is the call followed by a bare return.\n\nStill open: bound arguments are captured by reference, Haxe evaluates them at bind time (`var f = use.bind(a); a = 2; f()` prints 2 here, 7 in Haxe).","files":null}]}